## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: Fenton-like oxidation Fe(II) substitution Fe(III)/Fe(II) cycle Unsaturated metal centers Pollutant degradation
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), especially iron-based MOFs (Fe-MOFs), are deemed as promising Fenton-like catalysts due to their well-developed pores and accessible active sites. However, the Fenton-like performance of Fe-MOFs is limited by the relatively low redox rate of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ couples and density of coordinatively un­ saturated iron centers (CUICs), which depend on the intrinsic structure of iron-oxo nodes in Fe-MOFs. Herein, we manipulated the structure of iron-oxo nodes in MIL-53(Fe) via a $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ substitution method, affording a mixedvalence (i.e., Fe(II)/Fe(III)) material (denoted as $\mathrm{Fe^{II}{-}M I L{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe}))$ with highly improved Fenton-like perfor­ mance. The substituted Fe(II) centers could serve as stronger active sites, where $\mathrm{Fe(II){\rightarrow}F e(I I I)}$ half-reaction occurs, over original Fe(III) centers to rapidly activate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for efficient destruction of 4-nitrophenol (4-NP). Meanwhile, ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ substitution induced the formation of a larger amount of Fe(III) CUICs, which was proven by the increased Lewis acidity of MIL-53(Fe). The increased density of Fe(III) CUICs promoted the interaction be­ tween Fe(III) centers and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and thus rendered another half-reaction, $\mathrm{Fe(III){\to}F e(I I)}$ , with a greater rate. Accordingly, the accelerated cycle of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ couples favored •OH generation for improving 4-NP degra­ dation. As a result, the 4-NP degradation and rate on $\mathrm{Fe}^{\bar{\mathrm{II}}}.$ -MIL-53(Fe) were 1.39 and 9.48 times higher than that on pristine MIL-53(Fe). Moreover, $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}.$ -MIL-53(Fe) showed a good stability and reusability over multiple cycles. Our work provides insights into the rational design of Fe-MOFs as promising Fenton-like catalysts for advanced water/wastewater treatment.
## 1. Introduction
Water pollution (e.g., organic contaminations) has become one of the biggest challenges for both human survival and economic development [1–4]. It is imperative to explore highly efficient approaches for water purification to sustainably supply high-quality water for lives and in­ dustrial processes [5,6]. As one of the most representative and cost-effective advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) [7,8], heteroge­ neous Fenton oxidation technology is an attractive treatment option because it can destroy organic pollutants rather than divert them from waters enabled by the conventional adsorption or membrane processes [9,10]. Since the treatment efficiency of this technique highly depends on the category and structure of heterogeneous Fenton catalysts (HFCs) [11,12], much effort has been made to design and fabricate novel HFCs to effectively activate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ within a wide pH range (3.0–9.0) [13,14]. However, most of these HFCs, such as iron oxides and zero-valent irons, have some critical shortages toward engineering applications: (I) poor mass transfer property [15]; (II) low utilization ratio of active sites [16]; and (III) unacceptable stability under acidic conditions [17]. Therefore, exploring HFCs featured with well-developed pores, uniformly distrib­ uted accessible active sites, and high acid resistance is particularly important for practice.
