Recently, two important strategies have been explored to enhance the Fe(III)/Fe(II) redox cycle. One is the integration of external energy, particularly UV–vis/visible light, with Fe-MOFs catalyzed Fenton oxidation processes. In this strategy, Fe-MOFs characterizing with the semiconductor property can be excited to form charge carriers (e.g., electrons and holes) under UV or/and visible-light irradiation [28]. The generated electrons in the conduction band will deliver to Fe(III) in the oxo-node and promote the half reduction reaction (Eq. (1)) via a ligand-to-metal-charge-transfer process. As exemplified by Ai et al. work [29], the Fenton-like reactivity of MIL-53(Fe) (a representative Fe-MOF) for the organic pollutant destruction was enhanced in terms of the •OH yield and the apparent degradation rate constant by incorporating visible light into the Fenton-like oxidation process. However, the enhancement efficiency is probably limited by the poor quantum yield. Additionally, the need for specific equipment for producing useful light emission highly hinders its practical implementation. In contrast, the other strategy that relies on the structure regulation of Fe-MOFs is more feasible and promising to some extent. One specific route is to decrease the electron density of iron centers by introducing functional groups with strong electron-withdrawing ability. Recently, the Gao et al. [30] demonstrated that the •OH generation and Fe(III) reduction were highly improved on MIL-88B(Fe)-X $\mathrm{(X=NH_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , H, Br, and $\mathsf{N O}_{2})$ ). But the cost of special organic ligands applied in this method is too high to enable the large-scale production and application. Since the effective and continuing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation only occurs once the active sites–Fe(III) CUICs expose substantially, another specific route aims to create more amount of durable Fe(III) CUICs in Fe-MOFs. However, to the best of our knowledge, reports on the rational design/regulation of Fe-MOFs with desirable structures for improving Fe(III)/Fe(II) redox cycle are extremely scarce. As a typical illustration, vacuum activation of Fe-MOFs at temperatures as high as $230~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is found as a capable approach to producing more Fe(III) CUICs and Fe(II) centers. The increased Fe(III) CUICs would dynamically favor the reduction reaction (Eq. (1)) while another encountered oxidation reaction (Eq. (2)) could be promoted by the Fe(II) centers from the thermodynamic aspect [31]. Though the Fenton-like performance of Fe-MOFs is greatly improved through the enhanced overall cycle rate of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ redox, this approach suffers from energy-intensive and time-consuming problems. Furthermore, the obtained mixed-valence Fe-MOFs is unstable and need frequent re-activations after a few times of recycling. Accordingly, it is expected to rationally design and regulate Fe-MOFs for receiving abundant Fe(III) CUICs as well as Fe(II) centers by a more facile and cost-effective method.
In this study, we propose a Fe(II) substitution method to simply fabricate a mixed-valence MIL-53(Fe) (denoted as $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe)). By comparison to the fabrication procedure of MIL-53(Fe), Fe(II) substitu­ tion method contains an important step to manipulate the structure of iron-oxo nodes, in which partial Fe(III) precursor (i.e., $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0\dot{}.$ is replaced by Fe(II) salts (i.e., $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ . The effect of Fe(II) substitu­ tion on the physicochemical structure and Fenton-like reactivity of MIL53(Fe) was systematically studied. Specifically, the content of $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ CUICs and Fe(II) centers that determine the redox rate of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ couples in $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) was identified. The correlation between the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) structure and Fenton-like performance was investi­ gated. Moreover, the critical influencing factors (e.g., initial pH value, catalyst dosage, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration), reusability, and stability of $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) for 4-nitrophenol (4-NP) destruction were evaluated. Finally, the mechanism of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) for Fenton-like oxidation was disclosed by integrating the results of quenching experiments, EPR, and XPS. Our work offers a novel and facile approach to effectively improve the Fenton-like performance of Fe-MOFs through reasonably manipu­ lating the structure of iron-oxo nodes.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals
All the chemicals and reagents were of analytical grade and used as received. More details of used chemicals and reagents can be found in Text S1 in the Supporting Information. The involved ultrapure water (UW) was obtained from a Milli-Q Advantage A10 (Millipore).
## 2.2. Synthesis of $F e^{I I}–M I L–53(F e)$
The ${\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Fe})$ was synthesized using a ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ substitution method referred to a previous work with some adjustment [32]. Typi­ cally, $\ensuremath{3.784\mathrm{~g~}}$ 1 $\mathrm{\langle0.015~mol\rangle}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $1.189\mathrm{~g~}$ $\left(0.005\mathrm{\mol}\right)$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , and $3.32\mathrm{g}\left(0.02\mathrm{mol}\right)$ of $\mathrm{H_{2}B D C}$ were added into DMF (150 mL) to produce a precursor solution. The precursor solution was then transferred to a high-pressure autoclave containing a clean Teflon liner. The autoclave was maintained at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{h}$ . After naturally cooling down, the product was received by centrifugation and washing (several times with DMF ( $\mathrm{50~mL})$ , ethanol $\mathrm{50mL})$ , and deionized water $\ensuremath{\left(50\mathrm{mL}\right)}$ ) to remove the residual impurities. Afterward, the collected crude product was pre-dried in an oven ( $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{h}$ ) and then transferred to a vacuum oven for further drying ( $.100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24~\mathrm{h}\dot{}$ ). At last, an orange powder of ${\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}53({\mathrm{Fe}})$ was obtained and restored at a centrifuge tube. The pure MIL-53(Fe) was prepared using the same method without using $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ .
## 2.3. Characterization
The morphologies of prepared Fenton-like catalysts were detected by an Ultra 55 scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Zeiss, Germany). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were measured on a diffrac­ tometer (Thermo ARL-XTRA, Switzerland) at the 2θ ranging from 5 to $40^{\circ}$ . Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curve were collected on a TGA50 (SHIMADZU) thermogravimetric analyzer with a heating rate of 10 $^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$ under nitrogen atmosphere. The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra were recorded using a Vertex 70 spectrometer (Bruker, USA). An ASAP 2020 gas adsorption system (Micromeritics, USA) was employed to collect the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption-desorption isotherms of samples. The chemical state of surface elements in as-prepared catalysts was investigated by the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) with a Thermo Fisher Scientific K-Alpha instrument. The pyridine-chemisorbed FTIR (Py-FTIR) spectra were recorded using a Nicolet iS10 FTIR spec­ trometer (Thermo Scientific, USA). The details of Py-FTIR test can be found in the Text S3 in the Supporting Information.
## 2.4. Fenton-like reactivity test
All the Fenton-like reactivity tests were performed at a temperature of $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the dark. Specifically, $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) with a pre-set amount was added into a glass reactor $\mathrm{100~mL}$ containing 4-NP solution (50 $\mathrm{\mL},$ $20\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}$ . The initial $\mathsf{p H}$ value of the formed suspension was adjusted to 3–6 using 0.1 M HCl solution. Then, a certain $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ dosage was added into the suspension to induce the Fenton-like reaction. At different time intervals, $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of suspension was sampled using a sy­ ringe equipped with a polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) filter $(0.22\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . The samples were quenched by excessive n-butanol $\left[0.5\mathrm{mmol}\right]$ ) imme­ diately. For the subsequent cycle test, the spent catalyst was recovered by successive filtration using a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ PTFE filter, washing with UW and ethanol, drying at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , and then used again for the next run under the same conditions. Each Fenton-like reactivity test was carried out for triplicate.
## 2.5. Analysis methods
The 4-NP concentration, total organic carbon (TOC), the formed reactive oxygen species (ROS), •OH concentration, and iron leaching and intermediates during Fenton-like tests were systematically analyzed by various methods. More details of the employed analysis methods were listed in Text S2 in the Supporting Information.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structure characterizations of $F e^{{\cal I}{\cal I}}$ -MIL-53(Fe)
The morphologies of the mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) and pure MIL-53(Fe) were obtained from SEM images (Fig. 1). As illustrated in Fig. 1a, MIL-53(Fe) presented a regular octahedron crystallization shape with a particle size of about $1\times2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in length and width. While the introduction of Fe(II) altered the octahedron morphology of MIL-53(Fe) into a spindle-shaped one (Fig. 1b), whose particle size was about $10\times6$ $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in length and width. In addition, many cracks were formed on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe), which preferred to improve the mass transfer of reactants to accelerate the Fenton-like reaction [33]. The distinct morphologies between these two samples might be ascribed to the effect of $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ substitution on the MIL-53(Fe) crystal growth. To investigate the crystal phase of as-prepared catalysts, their PXRD patterns were collected and illustrated in Fig. 2. Although the intensity of peaks locating at $9.1^{\circ}$ and $18.4^{\circ}\mathbf{on}$ the pattern of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) was slightly stronger than that of MIL-53(Fe), both these two patterns matched quite well with the simulated one reported previously [34]. The differences in the intensity of visible peaks and the location of weak peaks at $15{\sim}20^{\circ}$ between recorded and simulated XRD patterns might be ascribed to the different reaction time used for MOF synthesis. In addition, no other impurity peaks appeared in the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ -MIL-53(Fe). $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}.$ ­ MIL-53(Fe) also displays an identical thermal gravimetric curve (Fig. S1) to pure MIL-53(Fe) [35]. The results indicate that $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) has a similar crystal phase and thermal stability with MIL-53(Fe), which promises its fundamental performance for advanced applications, such as Fenton-like oxidation.
The chemical structure (e.g., functional groups) of as-prepared samples were analyzed using FTIR spectroscopy (Fig. S2). As depicted in Fig. S2, both two FTIR spectra exhibited three characteristic peaks at 1663, 1590, and $1392~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , which were attributed to the stretching vibrations, asymmetric vibrations, and symmetric vibrations of $\mathtt{C=}0$ groups, respectively [20]. The peak corresponding to the bending vi­ brations of C–H groups in the benzene ring was also detected at 750 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The obvious peak at $548~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was ascribed to the Fe–O stretching vibrations of iron-oxo nodes. Since all these peaks were consistent well with those on the previous report [36], the presence of a dicarboxylate linker and the formation of iron-oxo clusters in the as-prepared samples could be testified. More importantly, $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}\mathrm{MIL}{-}53$ (Fe) showed a highly comparable FTIR spectrum to that of MIL-53(Fe), demonstrating that the effect of Fe(II) substitution on the functional groups of MIL-53(Fe) was negligible.
The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption (NAD) isotherms of mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}},$ -MIL-53(Fe) and pure MIL-53(Fe) are depicted in Fig. 3a. A typical type Ⅳ isotherm containing a hysteresis loop of H3 was clearly observed in the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms of both two catalysts, implying their mesoporous structure according to the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) classification [37]. Interestingly, mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) presented a bigger hyster­ esis loop than that of pure MIL-53(Fe). This result suggested that Fe(II) substitution could induce the generation more mesopores in $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53 (Fe), which favored the mass transfer property for enhancing the Fenton-like oxidation. To further identify the change of porosity, the pore size (PS) distribution of as-prepared samples is plotted in Fig. 3b. As shown in Fig. 3b, both of the samples possessed two distribution peaks between $2\mathrm{nm}$ and $100\mathrm{nm}$ , which confirmed the presence of mesopores. Moreover, the peak intensity in the case of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) was much stronger than that of MIL-53(Fe) due to the effect of Fe(II) substitution on the crystal growth, which agreed well with the result of morphology detection (Fig. 1). To gain a more direct comparison, the specific surface area (SSA), total pore volume (TPV), and average pore size (APS) of as-prepared catalysts are summarized in Table 1. The $\boldsymbol{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ , APS, and TPV of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) were determined to be $187.1\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $15.42\mathrm{nm}$ , and $0.497\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , whereas the corresponding values for MIL-53(Fe) were $157.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}^{2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , $6.70\mathrm{nm}$ , and $0.223\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. In addition, the increased $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ , TPV, and APS might derive from a fact that Fe(II) sub­ stitution can change the coordinated environment of MIL-53(Fe) and enable the generation of more CUICs. Therefore, more effective Fenton-like performance is highly expected as a result of the improved mass transfer property and the exposure of more CUICs. Note that the generation of more CUICs will be further elucidated by Py-FTIR spectra.
To further understand the effect of Fe(II) substitution on the fine chemical structure of MIL-53(Fe), we performed XPS measurements to disclose the surface compositions and electronic structures of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}},$ -MIL53(Fe) and MIL-53(Fe). The wide-scan XPS spectra shown in Fig. S3a revealed that both $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-53(Fe) mainly contained C, Fe, and O elements. As the enabled Fe(II) substitution of partial Fe(III) in the oxo-node allows to change the chemical environmental of iron centers, the high-resolution Fe 2p XPS spectra of pure MIL-53(Fe) and mixed-valence $\mathrm{\overline{{Fe}}^{I I}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) are recorded and illustrated in Fig. 4, respectively. For the former one, the peaks corresponding to Fe $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ (Fig. 4) could be observed at the binding energy (BE) of 711.1 and $725.0\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , respectively. The deconvoluted peaks at BEs of 711.3, 713.7, 724.8, and $727.8\mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to Fe(III) [24,31,38], whose fingerprint was furthered identified by two shakeup satellite peaks at BEs of 718.0 and 731.6 eV [24,39]. These findings indicated that iron element in the oxo-node of MIL-53(Fe) was mainly present as Fe(III) state [23,40]. However, the mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) generated new multiple peaks at 709.6 and $723.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ with a satellite signal at $715.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 4), excepting sharing the similar BEs for Fe (III)-related peaks with MIL-53(Fe). According to previous reports [31, 41], the generated new peaks corresponded to the Fe(II) species and thus confirmed the successful incorporation of Fe(II) in the oxo-node of $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe). Additionally, the location of corresponding Fe $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ peak in the Fe 2p XPS spectrum of $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) slightly shifted to a higher binding energy $(711.5\mathrm{eV})$ as compared that (711.1 eV) in the Fe 2p XPS spectrum of MIL-53(Fe). A higher binding energy means that the electrons are closer to the core, which indicates the lower electron density in Fe(III) centers [42–44]. To this end, this slight shift could be ascribed to the effect of Fe(II) on the electronic state of Fe(III) through inducing the transfer of more electron from Fe(III) centers to ligand part. The lower electron density around Fe(III) center in $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) is believed to show better affinity with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for enhancing the Fe(III)/Fe (II) cycle during Fenton-like process. Moreover, in Table 2, about 18.35 $\%$ of surface Fe(III) was substituted by Fe(II), which could favor, to some extent, the $\mathrm{Fe(II){\rightarrow}F e(I I I)}$ half-reaction. Therefore, the incorporation of Fe(II) into Fe-MOFs may make the mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) a promising Fenton-like candidate for organic pollutant degradation via accelerating the half-reaction of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycling.
The substitution of a different valence metal is believed to change the coordination environment of MOFs, which basically determines the exposure of active sites–CUICs. Since the active sites–CUICs can be alternatively analyzed by the Lewis acidity, the Py-FTIR spectroscopy was applied to investigate the influence of Fe(II) substitution on Fe(III) CUIC exposure. Fig. 5a and b displays the Py-FTIR spectra of MIL-53(Fe) and $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) at various degassing temperatures (i.e., $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 150 ${}^{\circ}\mathbf{C},$ and $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , respectively. The Py-FTIR spectra of MIL-53(Fe) showed two clear peaks at 1070 and $1042~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. 5a). They individually corresponded to the typical $\mathsf{v}_{18\mathrm{a}}$ and $\mathsf{v}_{12}$ vibration modes of Fe(III)-based Lewis acid sites (LAS) after pyridine sorption [45]. Unlike the Py-FTIR spectra of MIL-53(Fe), the corresponding peaks in the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) spectra shifted in varying degrees. The appearance of several distorted small peaks with lower wavenumbers (Fig. 5b) around the characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{v}_{18\mathrm{a}}$ and $\mathsf{v}_{12}$ vibration modes might belong to the Fe(II)-based LAS due to the introduction of weaker Lewis acid strength Fe(II) centers into oxo-nodes [46–48]. These observations demonstrated that effective Fe(II) substitution could alter the coordination environment of iron-oxo nodes within MIL-53(Fe) and thus probably promoted the exposure of Fe (III) CUICs. In order to quantify the amount of Fe(III) CUICs, we calcu­ lated the Lewis acidity of as-prepared catalysts as an alternative indi­ cation according to previous reports [46,49]. The area of peak at 1070 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and a molar absorption coefficient of $0.6\mathrm{cm}\upmu\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ were used to calculate the Lewis acidity. The amount of total, medium, and strong LAS within the MIL-53(Fe) were calculated to be 2.59, 2.11, and 1.29 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table 2). For the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe), the corre­ sponding LAS were determined to be 3.49, 2.49, and $0.85\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Obviously, $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) had a significantly increased amount over MIL-53(Fe) in terms of total and medium LAS. Further­ more, the amount of total and medium LAS within $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) were about 3.7 and 3.4 times higher than those in Bing et al. work [50]. Therefore, Fe(II) substitution could induce the formation of a larger amount of Fe(III) CUICs in MIL-53(Fe), which has been proven by the increased Lewis acidity of MIL-53(Fe) using the Py-FTIR measurement. Together with the porous structure and the presence of Fe(II) centers, we deduced that $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) could be a more promising catalyst for Fenton-like reaction than MIL-53(Fe).
## 3.2. Fenton-like performance of $F e^{{\cal I}{\cal I}}$ -MIL-53(Fe)
By using 4-NP as the model organic pollutant, the Fenton-like per­ formance of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) was assessed in the aspects of 4-NP degra­ dation, kinetic rate constant, and TOC removal. The changes of 4-NP concentration in various treatment systems are illustrated in Fig. 6a. Only $7.0\%$ of 4-NP was removed in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ alone, which indicated that 4-NP was hard to be destructed by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ without the catalyst. Meanwhile, $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) could adsorb $18.9\%$ of 4-NP due to its higher specific surface area, favoring the subsequent surface Fenton reactions through concentrating the targets. Whereas, only $14.8\%$ of 4- NP could be adsorbed onto MIL-53(Fe). Notably, the 4-NP degradation reached up $95.2~\%$ within $120\ \mathrm{min}$ in the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) catalyzed Fenton-like reaction system. implying that the 4-NP was preferably removed by the Fenton-like oxidation in the assistance of reactant in­ ward diffusion enhanced adsorption process. However, the degradation rate of 4-NP was only $68.5~\%$ for MIL-53(Fe) catalyst, much lower than that for $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ -MIL-53(Fe). Moreover, it was found that the Fenton-like performance of mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) outperformed pristine MIL-53(Fe) over a wide range of reaction conditions (Fig. S4) and other typical Fenton-like catalysts (Table S1) in terms of 4-NP degradation rate and kinetics. The result demonstrated that the incorporation of Fe(II) could substantially enhance the Fenton-like reactivity of MIL-53(Fe) regarding on 4-NP degradation. Together the physicochemical charac­ terization, Fe(II) substitution enabled the formation of mesopores accompanying with enlarged pores, so that the reactants, such as 4-NP, could easily diffuse into the internal structure for promoting the Fentonlike oxidation.
In addition, the mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}},$ -MIL-53(Fe) has a larger amount of Lewis acid sites over pure MIL-53(Fe), which is expected to provide more active sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation. Consequently, the interaction of active sites and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycle efficiency can be conducive to allow a more effective Fenton-like oxidation for the organic pollutant removal. More importantly, the construction of mixedvalence states in Fe-MOFs would promote •OH generation through accelerating the electron transfer between Fe(III) and Fe(II) species cycling, thus enhancing the Fenton-like performance for 4-NP degra­ dation [24]. These results demonstrated that the mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ ­ MIL-53(Fe) with higher porosity, a larger SSA, and more abundant LAS could be an ideal Fenton-like catalyst for the enhanced destruction of organic pollutants in water/wastewater treatment.
Since the reaction environment (e.g., initial pH value, catalyst dosage, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration) may pose unknown effects on the Fenton-like performance of the catalyst, systematical experiments were performed to evaluate the 4-NP degradation by $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}.$ -MIL-53(Fe) under various conditions. The results are displayed in Fig. 6b–d and the rele­ vant discussion is detailed in the Text S4 in Supporting Information. The 4-NP destruction obeyed a pseudo-first-order kinetic model over different reaction parameters (Fig. S5). The rapid 4-NP destruction stage was due to the presence of abundant unoccupied LAS in $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) and the high initial $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration at the very beginning of the Fenton-like reaction. More specifically, the ample of unoccupied LAS could capture $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ quickly to enable its homolytic cleavage, thereby generating concentrated •OH to destruct the target compound with a fast rate [51]. Not only is the surprising consumption of uninvolved LAS, but the reaction rate also depends on the regeneration rate of the valence transition state from Fe(III) to Fe(II) species [30,52].
During the Fenton-like oxidation process, the TOC value of with­ drawn samples was evaluated to analyze the mineralization of 4-NP. As described in Fig. S6, the TOC removal in the single presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was only $1.02\ \%$ , which suggested that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ could hardly enable the mineralization of 4-NP without a catalyst. Fortunately, the TOC removal in the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) catalyzed Fenton-like oxidation system reached up to $47.86\%$ while MIL-53(Fe) catalyst only obtained a TOC removal of $34.85~\%$ . The high increment $(13.01\ \%)$ of TOC removal in the case of $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) verified the excellent role of $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ substitution on improving the Fenton-like performance. Therefore, by the introduction of $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ , the purpose of increasing the degradation and mineralization rate of organic compound has been achieved. In addition, the ratio of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ dose $\left(R(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})\right)$ in relation to the stoichiometric amount (Eq. (S3) and Table S2) needed to achieve mineralization is about 5.0 based on the following equation: $R(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})=$ added $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ dose/theoretical $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ dose. The as-prepared $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) got a mineralization performance of $\ 23.99\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}\ H_{2}O_{2}},$ , which was comparable to other effective Fentonlike reactions (Table S3), indicating the excellent Fenton-like perfor­ mance of $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe).
## 3.3. Reusability and stability of $F e^{{\cal I}{\cal I}}$ -MIL-53(Fe)
Since the iron centers in the oxo-node plays a key role in the •OH generation and Fenton-like performance of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe), we used XPS technique to validate the change of its surface chemistry before and after use. It could be observed from Figs. S3b and 8 that the peak shapes of both vacant $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) and used $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) did not change significantly. Notably, the high-resolution Fe 2p XPS spectrum of used $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) showed that Fe(II) was still present, which could be attributed to the good cycling property of the mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe}$ (II) in the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) during the Fenton-like reaction. To give a direct comparison, the results of calculated Fe(II)/Fe(III) ratio is illus­ trated in Table S6. Interestingly, the surface Fe(II)/Fe(III) ratio (0.1835)
T. Yang et al.
of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}},$ -MIL-53(Fe) significantly increased after use (0.4273), which suggested that there was indeed a good redox cycle of $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ inside [58]. Moreover, the $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ ratio of mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ ­ MIL-53(Fe) before use was higher than that of pure MIL-53(Fe), infer­ ring that the origin $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ in the node of MIL-53(Fe) was successfully substituted by the Fe(II).
## 3.4. Possible catalysis mechanism
Since the reduction of $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ to $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ species by obtaining one electron from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is considered a rate-limiting step in heterogeneous
Fenton-like oxidation system [38,59,60], it can be expected that the Fe (II) substitution may improve the reducibility of Fe(III) through exposing more CUICs. To verify our speculation, the redox potentials of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ in as-prepared catalysts were determined by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) (Fig. 9a). The $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ redox potentials in pure MIL-53(Fe) and mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) were 0.561 V and 0.576 V, respectively. By comparison to pure MIL-53(Fe), the more positive Fe (III)/Fe(II) redox potential in mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) suggested that its Fe(III) species could be more easily reduced to Fe(II) [38]. Therefore, the acceleration of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycle for enhancing Fenton-like performance of Fe-MOFs can be facilely achieved by Fe(II) substitution. On the other hand, we also recorded the EIS Nyquist dia­ grams of pure MIL-53(Fe) and mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) to provide insights into the effect of Fe(II) substitution on the property of electron transfer. As shown in Fig. 9b, the arc radius in the EIS spectrum of mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) is much smaller than that of pure MIL-53 (Fe). This meant that electrons could be transferred from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to Fe(III) centers in $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ MIL-53(Fe) with a faster rate (see Eqs. (8) and (9) below), further clarifying the important role of Fe(II) substitution on enhancing the Fenton-like performance of Fe-MOFs.
The quenching experiments and EPR measurements were performed to investigate the mechanism of Fenton-like reaction using mixedvalence $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) as the efficient catalyst. It is well known that iron ions leached from bulk catalysts into reaction solution are viable substances that can catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to form free •OH $\mathrm{\dot{\mathrm{\Omega}}O H_{f r e e}})$ via homo­ geneous Fenton reaction. First of all, quenching experiments were con­ ducted using tert-Butanol and KI as the quenchers to confirm Fenton-like reaction type (e.g., homogeneous or heterogeneous) of 4-NP degrada­ tion. The tert-Butanol and KI were used because excess tert-Butanol can capture both the surface •OH $\mathrm{\cdot_{OH_{surf}}})$ and free •OH ${\mathrm{(}}{\mathrm{{-}}}_{\mathrm{{OH}}_{\mathrm{{free}}}}{\mathrm{)}}$ generated in the $\mathrm{Fe^{H_{-}}M I L}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ system, while iodide ion derived from KI only scavenges the $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{surf}}}$ at the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) catalyst [61,62]. As demonstrated in Fig. 10a, the degradation of 4-NP decreased remarkably from $95.2~\%$ to $23.7\ \%$ and $35.27\ \%$ in the case of tert-Bu­ tanol or KI addition, respectively. This observation revealed that the $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{surf}}}$ was responsible for 4-NP degradation, which meant that the Fenton-like reaction promoted by mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ -MIL-53(Fe) was heterogeneous one. Fig. 10b describes the EPR spectra of the formation of DMPO/•OH species in $\mathrm{Fe^{II}{-}M I L{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ Fenton-like system at 5 min. The signal of a four-fold special peak with an intensity ratio of 1:2:2:1 was clearly detected, identifying the present of •OH in the Fenton-like reaction solution [63,64]. More importantly, $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53 (Fe) had a stronger signal intensity than MIL-53(Fe), which could prove that $\mathrm{Fe^{H}{-}M I L{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ Fenton-like system produced more amount of •OH. This also further verified that the Fe(II) substitution had the ability to improve Fenton-like reactivity of pure MIL-53(Fe) via the promotion of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ redox cycling.
To quantify the •OH generation, we selected coumarin as probes for the determination of OH concentration with a fluorescence method ac­ cording to previous reports [65,66]. As shown in Fig. 11a and b, a distinct PL signal corresponding to 7- hydroxycoumarin (7-HC) in 450 nm could be clearly discovered. The intensity of this signal increased as reaction time prolonged for both two catalysts. Whereas, the PL signal of 7-HC in $\mathrm{Fe^{H}{-}M I L{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ system is much higher that of MIL-53 $\mathrm{(Fe)/H_{2}O_{2}}$ system. This suggested that $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) had a better ca­ pacity to effectively activate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into useful •OH for organic pollutant destruction. Since the intensity of the PL signal is positively correlated to •OH concentration, the •OH concentrations at various reaction times were determined [65,66]. Fig. 11c illustrates the results of determined •OH concentrations versus time. It could be found that the •OH concen­ tration in $\mathrm{Fe^{II}{-}M I L{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ system reached to as high as ${\sim}8~\upmu\mathrm{M};$ , which was 1.40 times over that in $\mathrm{Fe^{II}{-}M I L{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ system. The result demonstrated that ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ substitution could enable the generation of more •OH for effective organic pollutant removal in the Fe-MOFs based Fenton-like oxidation system.
Based on the obtained results and previous studies, a possible catalysis mechanism of $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation and 4-NP degradation was proposed and demonstrated in Fig. 12. On the one hand, $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ -MIL-53(Fe) having porous and open network was allowed to improve the mass transfer of reactants into the catalytically active sites. As a result, the active sites–Lewis acid sites in the mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ MIL-53(Fe) became more accessible over pure MIL-53(Fe). The 4-NP molecules were adsorbed on pore channel of $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ -MIL-53(Fe) probably via the $\pi-\pi$ interaction between the benzene ring of 4-NP and organic linkers in $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MIL-53(Fe), anchoring 4-NP molecules onto the inner surface of ${\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})$ for further degradation dominated by the heterogeneous surface reactions. On the other hand, the functional complexes $(e.g.,\equiv_{\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}-\mathrm{MOF}-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ an $1\equiv_{\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}-\mathrm{MOF-H_{2}O_{2}}})$ were rapidly generated once $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was added due to the strong affinity between the Lewis acid sites and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules (Eqs. (5) and (6) [67]. Afterwards, the $\equiv\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -MOF- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ complex inclined to form $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdot}}_{\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{surf}}}$ and $\equiv\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MOF species through the intramolecular electron transfer (Eq. (7)). On the other hand, the $\equiv\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MOF species (e.g., $\equiv_{\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}-\mathrm{MOF}-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ and $\equiv\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ -MOF) could be primarily reduced to $\equiv\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MOF species via these reactions (Eqs. (8) and (9)). The $\equiv_{\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}-\mathrm{MOF}}$ species and $\equiv\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -MOF species were constantly transferred to each other thereupon, producing more amount of $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{surf}}}$ via the accelerated $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ redox cycle. The produced $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{surf}}}$ could effectively destruct 4-NP molecules near Lewis acid sites (Eq. (10)). Furthermore, the instantaneously concen­ trated 4-NP and $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{surf}}}$ in the microenvironment could become a driving force to further promote 4-NP degradation at very beginning. Note that the leached iron ions from $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ -MIL-53(Fe) might also contribute slightly to 4-NP degradation due to the production of $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{free}}}$ from the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition through a chain reaction (Eqs. (11)–(14)).
