## 3.5. Degradation pathway of 4-NP
Gas chromatography with tandem mass spectrometry (GC–MS) and mass spectrometry (MS) were used to unveil the degradation in­ termediates of 4-NP, which probably favor the 4-NP destruction mech­ anism in the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}{-}M I L{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ Fenton-like system. Six intermediates, including acetate $(m/z=59.0)$ , propionic acid $(m/z=$ 74.1), ethanedioic acid $(m/z=91.0)\$ ), propanedioic acid $(m/z=140.0)$ , succinic Acid $(m/z=119.0)$ , and $p$ -benzoquinone $(m/z=107.0)$ (Fig. S10 in the Supporting Information), were clearly detected by GC–MS.
In addition, other intermediates were also identified by MS, such as 1- hydroxy-propanoic acid $(m/z=90.101\mathrm{\cdot}$ ), glycerol $(m/z=92.902)\$ , tar­ tronic acid $(m/z=119.971)$ , pyrogallol or 1,2,4-benzenetriol $(m/z=$ 128.760), and 3-hydroxymuconic acid or 2-hydroxymuconic acid $(m/z$ $=157.707)$ (Fig. S11 in the Supporting Information). On the basis of identified intermediates and previous studies [68,69], we proposed a possible degradation pathway of 4-NP in the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}{-}M I L{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$
Fenton-like system in Fig. 13. At first, the electrophilic addition of •OH to 4-NP resulted in the formation of $p$ -benzoquinone and $p$ -nitrocatechol. Afterwards, the reactivity of these intermediates with the hydroxyl group in the ortho and para positions was largely enhanced. In other words, they had low stability and preferred to subject ring-opening oxidation reaction, which further went to small organic acids. Eventu­ ally these acids were destructed into $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and inorganic ions by
•OH.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we demonstrated for the first time that partial Fe(II) substitution of Fe(III) centers in pure MIL-53(Fe) could substantially enhance its Fenton-like performance. After Fe(II) substitution, the mass transfer property and the amount of active sites of MIL-53(Fe) were improved to contribute to the degradation of 4-NP with higher rate and mineralization. More importantly, the presence of Fe(II) centers and the increased Fe(III) CUICs accelerate the Fe(III)/Fe(II) redox cycle, a ratelimited step in Fenton-like oxidation, to continuously generate •OH via electron transfers, leading to a 9.84-fold and 1.39-fold increment in terms of 4-NP degradation rate constant and TOC removal, respectively. By comparison to other strategies, the Fe(II) substitution method is found to be more facile and feasible for scaling up and real applications. This study sheds new lights for the rational design of Fe-MOFs as promising Fenton-like catalysts for advanced water/wastewater treatment.
